PLACES=[
N('fort-washita-ok','Fort Washita','Durant','ok','fort','ruins',34.1023,-96.54699,'site','open','walk','forts','1842–1865',
  'Zachary Taylor founded the post in 1842 to guard the newly removed Choctaw and Chickasaw from Plains raids; its south barracks went up in limestone in 1849 and the west barracks in 1856. Federal troops left at the start of the Civil War, Confederates held it, and they burned what was left when they went in 1865. The Colbert family farmed the ground and lived in the west barracks until it burned in 1917. The Oklahoma Historical Society bought the site in 1962 and rebuilt the south barracks in 1971; the Chickasaw Nation bought it in 2017, and in 2023 it was put in federal trust.',
  'The stone walls of the west barracks, a ruin since the 1917 fire, and the rebuilt south barracks beside them (Wikipedia has a 2009 photograph of the pair), with a Confederate cemetery on the grounds. The rebuilt south barracks burned on September 26, 2010; whether it has been rebuilt again was not checked. Other restored buildings can be toured (Chickasaw Nation page); which ones was not checked.',
  'Confederates burned it on the way out, and a farm family lived in the ruin.',
  'Owned and run by the Chickasaw Nation, 3348 State Highway 199, about 16 miles west of Durant. Two Chickasaw Nation pages read November 21, 2026 give the closing time as 4:30 p.m. (location page) and 4 p.m. (services page). Group tours must be booked ahead. Parking at the site, spot not checked.',
  [W('Fort Washita'),['Chickasaw Nation: Fort Washita','https://chickasaw.net/fortwashita'],['Chickasaw Nation: Fort Washita (services page)','https://www.chickasaw.net/Services/Culture/Fort-Washita']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. (one Chickasaw page says 4 p.m.); closed Sunday and Monday'),
N('fort-towson-ok','Fort Towson Historic Site','Fort Towson','ok','fort','ruins',34.02799,-95.25855,'site','open','walk','forts','1824–1865',
  'Cantonment Towson was set up in May 1824 on Gates Creek near the Red River to watch the Spanish border, and given up in 1829. In 1830 the Army ordered a permanent post nearby for the Choctaw being moved west, and it was named Fort Towson in 1831: log quarters on limestone foundations round a half-acre parade. After Fort Washita was built the garrison left in 1854 and the Choctaw agency took over; a storm and then a fire destroyed all but one barracks and the hospital. Confederate General Stand Watie surrendered here on June 23, 1865, and afterwards local people hauled away the foundation stones.',
  'Foundations of the fort on the bluff, reached by interpretive walking trails from a visitor center with exhibits and a sutler store (Oklahoma Historical Society). How much of each building\'s footing survives was not checked.',
  'Where the last Confederate general in the field gave up.',
  'Run by the Oklahoma Historical Society, 896 N 4375 Road, Fort Towson. Admission $10 adults, $8 seniors, $5 students, members and veterans free; closed November 26 and 27 and December 24 to 26, 2026 (society\'s visit page, read November 21, 2026). Drones need the director\'s written approval. Parking at the visitor center, spot not checked.',
  [W('Fort Towson'),['Oklahoma Historical Society: Fort Towson','https://www.okhistory.org/sites/forttowson'],['Oklahoma Historical Society: plan your visit',OHS+'visit-fort-towson']],
  brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),
N('doaksville-ok','Doaksville archaeological site','Fort Towson','ok','town','traces',34.03379,-95.27333,'area','public','walk','','1820s–1903',
  'A Choctaw town founded between 1824 and 1831 round Josiah Doak\'s trading post, next to Fort Towson. By 1850 it was the largest town in Indian Territory, with stores, a mill, a hotel and two newspapers, and it was the Choctaw capital until 1859 (Wikipedia also says 1860 to 1863; the article gives both). A railroad passed it by in 1870 and the post office closed in 1903. The Oklahoma Historical Society bought the site in 1960, dug it in 1995 to 1997, and in 2001 laid a walkway with signs past the uncovered foundations.',
  'A trail with signs past the foundations of several buildings found in the 1990s digs, beside the Fort Towson Doaksville Cemetery. Wikipedia says in one place that nothing but the cemetery remains and in another that the foundations can be seen; how much shows today was not checked.',
  '',
  'Oklahoma Historical Society site, run from Fort Towson Historic Site. From US 70 in the town of Fort Towson turn north on Red Road for 0.7 mile and into Fort Towson Cemetery; the trailhead parking sign is there and the trail starts up steps over the cemetery\'s east wall (society\'s visit page, read November 21, 2026). Hours for the trail not given.',
  [W('Doaksville, Choctaw Nation'),['Oklahoma Historical Society: directions to Doaksville',OHS+'visit-fort-towson']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('fort-gibson-ok','Fort Gibson Historic Site','Fort Gibson','ok','fort','standing',35.80699,-95.25333,'site','open','drive','forts','1824–1888',
  'Colonel Matthew Arbuckle set up Cantonment Gibson on April 21, 1824 on the Grand River near where it meets the Arkansas, then the farthest west of any US Army post. It became Fort Gibson in 1832 and for years dealt with the Cherokee and other peoples driven west on removal and with their quarrels with the Osage; Washington Irving and George Catlin both passed through. It guarded the frontier until 1888. In the 1930s the government rebuilt the early log fort as a Depression work project.',
  'The 1930s reconstruction of the log fort, original buildings from the 1840s to the 1870s, and the Commissary Visitor Center with exhibits (Wikipedia). Which original buildings can be entered was not checked; the society\'s page lists a historic hospital and construction works on the site.',
  '',
  'Run by the Oklahoma Historical Society, 803 North Garrison Avenue, Fort Gibson. Admission $10 adults, $8 seniors, $5 students, members and veterans free; closed November 26 and 27, December 11 and 12 and December 24 to 26, 2026 (society\'s visit page, read November 21, 2026). Parking at the site, spot not checked.',
  [W('Fort Gibson'),['Oklahoma Historical Society: Fort Gibson',OHS+'fortgibson'],['Oklahoma Historical Society: plan your visit',OHS+'visit-fort-gibson']],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),
N('fort-supply-ok','Fort Supply Historic Site','Fort Supply','ok','fort','standing',36.573,-99.5527,'area','view','drive','forts','1868–1894',
  'Camp Supply was set up on November 18, 1868 for Sheridan\'s winter campaign; Custer rode south from here to attack Black Kettle\'s village on the Washita. Renamed Fort Supply in 1878, it closed in 1894 when the Cherokee Outlet was opened to settlement. In 1908 the old post became Oklahoma\'s first asylum, and in 1988 the state named the surviving buildings a historic district, beside what became the William S. Key Correctional Center.',
  'Five original buildings being restored by the Oklahoma Historical Society: the 1874 ordnance sergeant\'s quarters and 1882 civilian employee quarters, both picket-style log, the frame 1878 commanding officer\'s quarters and 1882 officers\' duplex, and the brick 1892 guard house (Wikipedia). The site is shut for now: look from the public road only.',
  '',
  'The Oklahoma Historical Society\'s page, read November 21, 2026, says the site is closed until further notice, tied to the Key Correctional Facility next door, and will reopen with the Friends of Historic Fort Supply. The pin is the address-search point for the historic site and was not matched to a building.',
  [W('Fort Supply (Oklahoma)'),['Oklahoma Historical Society: Fort Supply',OHS+'fortsupply']],
  brick='?'),
N('fort-reno-ok','Historic Fort Reno','El Reno','ok','fort','standing',35.56136,-98.03382,'site','open','drive','forts','1874–1949',
  'A cavalry post begun in July 1874 beside the Darlington Agency during the trouble that became the Red River War, kept to watch the Cheyenne and Arapaho reservation and the Boomers; the Ninth Cavalry\'s Buffalo Soldiers served here. After 1908 it raised horses and mules as a remount depot. In the Second World War German prisoners, most taken in North Africa, were held here and built the chapel. The Army left in 1949 (Wikipedia; the National Park Service gives 1947 for the remount station\'s closing) and the Department of Agriculture runs it as a grazing research station.',
  'Buildings round the parade ground, brick ones from the 1880s, old sheds and quarters, the prisoner-built chapel, and a rock-walled post cemetery with German and Italian prisoners\' graves (National Park Service page). The visitor center museum shows artifacts, prisoners\' letters and Remington art. The U.S. Cavalry Association Museum on the grounds charges separately.',
  'A cavalry post where Afrika Korps prisoners built the chapel.',
  'Federal land run as a research station; the visitor center is Historic Fort Reno, Inc.\'s, 7107 West Cheyenne Street, on old Route 66 four miles west of El Reno. Museum $6 adults, $5 seniors and military, $4 children 5 to 17. Closed federal holidays except Memorial Day and Veterans Day and whenever the government shuts; the gate closes itself at 4:30 p.m. (its site, read November 21, 2026). Lot beside the visitor center.',
  [W('Fort Reno (Oklahoma)'),['Historic Fort Reno, Inc.','https://fortreno.org/'],['National Park Service: Fort Reno','https://www.nps.gov/places/oklahoma-fort-reno.htm']],
  th=[35.56094,-98.03336],thn='Visitor center lot',conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; winter hours may vary'),
N('arcadia-round-barn','Arcadia Round Barn','Arcadia','ok','landmark','standing',35.66236,-97.32598,'exact','open','drive','','1898',
  'Farmer William Harrison Odor built the barn in 1898 from bur oak boards soaked green and bent to the curve of the walls and rafters, with a loft above for gatherings. Route 66 was laid past it in the 1920s; when the interstate took the traffic away, barn and town declined, and the 60-foot roof fell in in 1988. Volunteers led by Luther Robison rebuilt it by 1992.',
  'A truly round barn (not many-sided) beside old Route 66. Downstairs is a museum and gift shop with farm tools, a railroad baggage cart and Route 66 relics; the domed loft with its hardwood floor, once a dance hall, is reached by outside stairs and often rented in the evenings. A free guided tour runs Saturdays at 1 p.m. (its page).',
  '',
  'Run by the Arcadia Historical and Preservation Society, 107 East Highway 66, six miles east of Interstate 35. Free, donations asked. Hours may change with volunteers or weather (its page, read November 21, 2026). Parking at the barn, spot not checked.',
  [W('Arcadia Round Barn'),['Arcadia Round Barn: plan your visit','https://arcadiaroundbarn.com/?p=619'],['National Park Service: Arcadia Round Barn','https://www.nps.gov/places/arcadia-round-barn.htm']],
  brick='no',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; free tour Saturday 1 p.m.'),
N('marland-mansion-ponca-city','Marland Mansion','Ponca City','ok','landmark','standing',36.71698,-97.06065,'exact','open','drive','','1925–1928',
  'Oil man E. W. Marland built this "Palace on the Prairie" at the height of the 1920s oil boom, to John Duncan Forsyth\'s design after the Palazzo Davanzati in Florence. It stands in an old stone quarry whose pit became the swimming pool, and its limestone was cut on the spot. It is a National Historic Landmark and a museum.',
  'A 55-room limestone house on a steel frame under red clay tile, with a porte-cochère guarded by carvings of Marland\'s four hunting dogs by Ernest Pellegrini, a carved Pan spout dated 1927, and a cantilevered stone stair on the north front (Wikipedia). Self-guided tours inside.',
  '',
  'Marland Mansion, 901 Monument Road, Ponca City. Adults $12, seniors $10, children 6 to 17 and military $5, under 6 free. Last self-guided tour starts at 3:30 p.m.; closed on major holidays (its page lists them; one list says Good Friday, the other Easter). Lot on Monument Road by the house.',
  [W('E. W. Marland Mansion'),['Marland Mansion: visitor information','https://marlandmansion.com/information.html']],
  th=[36.71632,-97.06154],thn='Lot on Monument Road',brick='no',hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; last tour 3:30 p.m.'),
N('sod-house-cleo-springs','Sod House Museum (Marshal McCully sod house)','Aline','ok','landmark','standing',36.47218,-98.42569,'site','open','drive','','1894',
  'Marshal McCully took part in the Cherokee Outlet land run of September 16, 1893 and built this house of sod the next year, as settlers did on the treeless plains. It is the only sod house built by settlers left in Oklahoma (Wikipedia), listed on the National Register in 1970.',
  'The 1894 sod house itself, enclosed inside the museum building, with exhibits on homestead farming (Oklahoma Historical Society).',
  'The last settlers\' sod house in Oklahoma, kept under a roof.',
  'Run by the Oklahoma Historical Society, 4628 State Highway 8, Aline, about four miles north of Cleo Springs. Admission $10 adults, $8 seniors, $5 students. Staff are few, so the society asks you to telephone before going; closed November 26 and 27 and December 24 and 25, 2026 (visit page, read November 21, 2026). Parking at the museum, spot not checked.',
  [W('Sod House (Cleo Springs, Oklahoma)'),['Oklahoma Historical Society: Sod House Museum',OHS+'sodhouse'],['Oklahoma Historical Society: plan your visit',OHS+'visit-sod-house']],
  brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; call ahead'),
N('hunters-home-park-hill','Hunter\'s Home (George M. Murrell Home)','Park Hill','ok','landmark','standing',35.85562,-94.95903,'exact','open','drive','','1845',
  'George Murrell, a white planter married to Minerva Ross, niece of Cherokee chief John Ross, built the house in 1845 and named it for his love of the fox hunt. The family held 42 enslaved people in nine cabins here. Raiders of both sides swept the area in the Civil War, but the house was the only local building to come through. The state bought it in 1948, and it is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'A house furnished as in the 1830s to 1860s with Murrell family pieces, and the Daniel Cabin, a log cabin used for demonstrations of Cherokee life in the 1850s (Wikipedia).',
  'The one building round Park Hill the Civil War spared.',
  'Run by the Oklahoma Historical Society, 19479 East Murrell Home Road, Park Hill, south of Tahlequah. Admission $10 adults, $8 seniors, $5 students. No pets. Closed November 26 to 28, December 10 and 12 and December 24 to 26, 2026 (visit page, read November 21, 2026). Parking at the house, spot not checked.',
  [W('Murrell Home'),['Oklahoma Historical Society: Hunter\'s Home',OHS+'huntershome'],['Oklahoma Historical Society: plan your visit',OHS+'visit-hunters-home']],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),
N('overholser-mansion-okc','Overholser Mansion','Oklahoma City','ok','landmark','standing',35.48516,-97.51952,'exact','open','drive','','1903',
  'Henry Overholser, often called the father of Oklahoma City, and his wife Anna built the city\'s first mansion out among farm fields; it opened with a gala in 1904. W. S. Matthews, trained in London, designed it in the French château style. Senator Mike Monroney lived in it from 1956 to 1968, and the Oklahoma Historical Society acquired it in 1972; Preservation Oklahoma runs it.',
  'Buff brick and red sandstone with a three-story octagonal corner tower. Inside, hand-painted canvas walls and ceilings, the original French stained glass and the family\'s furniture (society\'s page and Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'At the northwest corner of North Hudson Avenue and NW 15th Street, Heritage Hills. Run by Preservation Oklahoma for the Oklahoma Historical Society. Closed for restoration; the society\'s page, read November 21, 2026, expects to reopen in 2027. Street parking, not checked.',
  [W('Overholser Mansion'),['Oklahoma Historical Society: Overholser Mansion',OHS+'overholsermansion']],
  brick='no',hours='Closed for restoration; reopening expected in 2027'),
N('choctaw-capitol-tuskahoma','Choctaw Capitol (Tvshkahomma Capitol Museum)','Tuskahoma','ok','civic','standing',34.64262,-95.27626,'site','open','drive','','1884',
  'After years of moving its capital about, the Choctaw National Council in 1883 ordered a permanent seat at Tushka Homma, "home of the red warrior". The capitol was finished in September 1884 of red native brick, sandstone and local timber for $30,000, with rooms for the Senate, House, Principal Chief and Supreme Court. It served until statehood in 1907, then fell into disrepair; the nation bought it back within a decade of losing it in the 1920s, and opened it as a museum in 1975.',
  'A two-story red brick building with a mansard attic, 73 by 62 feet. Inside, exhibits on the capitol, on Choctaw schools before the boarding schools and on the Code Talkers, and a gift shop; outside, a veterans\' memorial and walking paths (Choctaw Nation page).',
  '',
  'Choctaw Nation of Oklahoma, Council House Road, about two miles north of Tuskahoma. Free. Tours of the capitol and grounds can be arranged (its page, read November 21, 2026). The Labor Day festival here draws very large crowds. Lots on Council House Road, spot not checked.',
  [W('Choctaw Capitol Building'),['Choctaw Nation: Tvshkahomma Capitol Museum','https://www.choctawnation.com/about/culture/tvshkahomma-capitol-museum/']],
  brick='yes',hours='Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('wheelock-academy-ok','Wheelock Academy Historic Site','Millerton','ok','civic','standing',33.99256,-94.98803,'site','open','drive','','1832–1955',
  'Alfred Wright, a Presbyterian missionary doctor who came west with the Choctaw, began a school here in 1832 and named it for Eleazar Wheelock; it became the first Choctaw national academy in 1842, a girls\' boarding school where pupils were forbidden their own language. It shut in the Civil War and partly burned in 1869; the Choctaw Nation rebuilt it in 1880 to 1884. It became a U.S. Indian School in 1932 and closed for good in 1955.',
  'Seven buildings stand, most in poor condition as of a 2001 report (Wikipedia); all were painted and reroofed by 1999. LeFlore Hall has been refurbished as a museum of photographs and students\' belongings (Choctaw Nation page).',
  'The model for the boarding schools of the Five Tribes, half of it falling in.',
  'Choctaw Nation of Oklahoma, about three miles east of Millerton. Tours of the museum and grounds on request: telephone the site (Choctaw Nation page, read November 21, 2026; no days or times given). Parking at the campus, spot not checked.',
  [W('Wheelock Academy'),['Choctaw Nation: Wheelock Academy Historic Site','https://choctawnationculture.com/wheelock/wheelock-academy-historic-site.aspx']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('wheelock-church-ok','Wheelock Mission Church','Millerton','ok','worship','standing',33.99056,-94.99028,'site','view','drive','','1845–1846',
  'Alfred Wright preached his first sermon here under an oak in December 1832, using a barrel for a pulpit. The stone church, built in 1845 and 1846 mostly by Choctaw volunteers, is the oldest church building standing in Oklahoma. A fire in 1866 took its roof, which was not replaced until 1884, and regular services stopped at the end of 1897. Wright is buried in the cemetery across the road.',
  'Thick stone walls, a vaulted ceiling over the floor and balcony, and Wright\'s motto "Jehovah Jireh" carved in the gable, said to be still legible (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'The oldest church building in Oklahoma.',
  'Near Wheelock Academy, on Choctaw Nation land east of Millerton. Whether it is opened on the academy\'s tours was not checked: look from the road. The pin is the Wikipedia point, not matched to the building.',
  [W('Wheelock Church')],
  brick='no'),
N('boggy-depot-ok','Boggy Depot','Atoka','ok','town','traces',34.3216,-96.3136,'area','public','walk','','1837–1934',
  'Choctaw and Chickasaw people founded the town in 1837 on the Texas Road; it got a post office in 1848 and became a Butterfield Overland stage stop in 1858. Its church was the Choctaw capitol for a time in 1859, it was a Confederate supply depot in the Civil War, and Choctaw Chief Allen Wright, who lived here, coined the word "Oklahoma" in 1866. The Katy railroad passed it by in 1872, Atoka took its trade, and the post office closed in 1934.',
  'A few stone foundations and the cemetery, where Allen Wright and the missionary Cyrus Kingsbury are buried, inside Boggy Depot Park, which has a fishing lake and a nature trail (Wikipedia). Where the foundations are in the park was not checked.',
  '',
  'Boggy Depot Park, 475 South Park Lane, Atoka: once a state park, handed to a tribe when the state closed it (Wikipedia says the Choctaw in one place and the Chickasaw in another; TravelOK, the state tourism site, says the Chickasaw Nation manages it and that it is open year round). The manager\'s own page was not read. The pin is the park\'s mapped point.',
  [W('Boggy Depot, Oklahoma'),['TravelOK: Boggy Depot Park','https://www.travelok.com/state-parks/670']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('medicine-park-ok','Medicine Park cobblestone town','Medicine Park','ok','town','standing',34.72699,-98.50077,'area','public','drive','','1908',
  'Elmer Thomas, a new state senator and later a U.S. senator, opened a resort here on July 4, 1908, at first a surplus Army tent serving meals beside two small dams on Medicine Creek. Inns, a dance hall and a swimming hole followed; Bob Wills and the Texas Playboys played the dance hall from 1929 into the late 1930s. Many of its first buildings are of the red granite cobblestones found only in the Wichita Mountains.',
  'A small town of cobblestone buildings on Medicine Creek at the edge of the Wichita Mountains Wildlife Refuge (Wikipedia). Which buildings are original and what is in them was not checked.',
  '',
  'A town of about 400 people; public streets. Town office open Monday to Friday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. (town site, read November 21, 2026). The pin is the building OpenStreetMap names Cobblestone Row, on East Lake Drive.',
  [W('Medicine Park, Oklahoma'),['Town of Medicine Park','https://www.medicinepark.com/']],
  brick='?'),
N('cherokee-national-capitol','Cherokee National Capitol (Cherokee National History Museum)','Tahlequah','ok','civic','standing',35.91258,-94.97053,'exact','open','drive','','1867–1869',
  'After most of Tahlequah\'s public buildings were lost in the Civil War, the Cherokee Nation built this capitol in 1867 to 1869 to C. W. Goodlander\'s design. It held the nation\'s council and courts until statehood in 1907 abolished the tribal government, and was made a National Historic Landmark in 1961. Restored from 2013, it reopened in 2019 as the Cherokee National History Museum.',
  'A two-story red brick Italianate building with white wood trim, pedimented gables and arched windows, in the middle of Courthouse Square (Wikipedia). Inside, over 7,000 square feet of Cherokee history and art (Cherokee Nation page).',
  '',
  'Cherokee Nation, 101 South Muskogee Avenue, Tahlequah. Free (its page, read November 21, 2026). Lots round the square.',
  [W('Cherokee National Capitol'),['Visit Cherokee Nation: the museum','https://visitcherokeenation.com/attractions/cherokee-national-history-museum/']],
  th=[35.91249,-94.97007],thn='Lot on South Water Avenue',brick='yes',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('coleman-theatre-miami-ok','Coleman Theatre','Miami','ok','civic','standing',36.87644,-94.87774,'exact','open','drive','','1929',
  'Mining magnate George Coleman built the theatre on Route 66 and opened it on April 18, 1929, billed as the finest between Dallas and Kansas City, for films, vaudeville and a ten-piece orchestra. The Boller Brothers of Kansas City designed it. The Coleman family gave it to the City of Miami in 1989, and volunteers restored it.',
  'A Spanish Revival stucco front with a curving gable, carved terra cotta and bell towers; inside, Louis XV decoration, the original chandelier restored, winding stairs with gilded statues, and the 1928 "Mighty Wurlitzer" organ (National Park Service page). It offers tours and shows (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Miami and run by the Coleman Theatre, 103 North Main Street. Its own site answered 403, so tour times were not read. Street parking, not checked.',
  [W('Coleman Theatre'),['National Park Service: Coleman Theatre','https://www.nps.gov/places/coleman-theatre.htm']],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('boston-avenue-methodist-tulsa','Boston Avenue Methodist Church','Tulsa','ok','worship','standing',36.14384,-95.98437,'exact','view','drive','','1929',
  'A Methodist church in downtown Tulsa, completed in 1929, whose design is credited to the art teacher Adah Robinson and her former pupil Bruce Goff; who did more is still argued. It is counted among the finest Art Deco churches in the country and was made a National Historic Landmark in 1999.',
  'A semicircular auditorium and a 225-foot tower of Indiana limestone and Minnesota granite, with Robert Garrison\'s terra cotta figures: praying hands on the pinnacles, mounted circuit riders over the south door and the Wesleys over the north; the stained glass takes its patterns from Oklahoma flowers (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church at 1301 South Boston Avenue. Its own site answered 403, so service and tour times were not read: look from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Boston Avenue Methodist Church')],
  brick='no'),
]
